The Sighurt contributors have modified the files that came from Oxide. The
codebase has been restructured into a browser core (sighurt-core), a Wayland
host (sighurt-wayland), a chrome drawn on the CPU (sighurt-ui) and the sig
executable (sighurt-browser), with the content engines compiled into sig. The
WASM engine (sighurt-engine-wasm), the guest SDK (sighurt-sdk, formerly
oxide-sdk), sig's downloads, the documentation and the example apps are
derived from Oxide's source. The git history records the individual changes;
the code before the restructuring is at the git tag gpui-baseline. Sighurt was
relicensed from Apache-2.0 to AGPL-3.0-or-later after commit 0d052a7.

The WASM guest ABI keeps Oxide's "oxide" import module name, so applications
built for Oxide run unchanged in Sighurt's WASM engine.

Exceptions:

- sighurt-sdk, the guest SDK compiled into WASM applications, and the example
  applications in examples/ stay under the Apache License, Version 2.0, like
  Oxide, so applications built with them may use any license.

- sighurt-engine-servo, the Servo engine compiled into sig, is licensed under
  AGPL-3.0-or-later AND MPL-2.0. It contains no files adapted from the Servo
  project (https://github.com/servo/servo) at present; any that it adapts keep
  their notices and remain under the Mozilla Public License, Version 2.0
  (https://mozilla.org/MPL/2.0/). sig built with the engine-servo feature (one
  of its default features) also links Servo, which is licensed under MPL-2.0.

- third_party/surfman is surfman 0.14.0 (https://github.com/servo/surfman), the
  version Servo 0.7 uses, under MIT OR Apache-2.0 OR MPL-2.0 (its LICENSE-MIT,
  LICENSE-APACHE and LICENSE-MPL). One patch, recorded in
  third_party/surfman/SIGHURT-PATCH.md: `create_context_descriptor` no longer
  calls `Adapter::set_environment_variables`, which wrote
  `LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE` and `DRI_PRIME` after other threads existed.

- sig built with the engine-wpe feature loads WPE WebKit's libraries
  dynamically; they keep their own licenses (LGPL-2.1 and BSD).
